tructure: `construct_mapping` returns `DictToken(mapping, start, end - 1, content=str_content)` where `mapping = loader.construct_mapping(node)`. Since the custom constructors are registered, the nested values are Tokens (ScalarToken etc.), not plain Python values. So `token.value` is `{ScalarToken(\"key\",0,2): ScalarToken(\"value\",5,9)}`.\n\nWait, is that right? `loader.construct_mapping(node)` \u2192 for each key/value node, calls `self.construct_object(key_node, ...)` which uses the registered constructor \u2192 returns ScalarToken. So yes, the dict keys/values are Tokens.\n\nHmm, but wait: `construct_mapping` in SafeLoader \u2014 does it deep-construct? `construct_mapping(self, node, deep=False)`: \n\n```python\ndef construct_mapping(self, node, deep=False):\n    if not isinstance(node, MappingNode):\n        raise ConstructorError(...)\n    mapping = {}\n    for key_node, value_node in node.value:\n        key = self.construct_object(key_node, deep=deep)\n        if not isinstance(key, collections.abc.Hashable):\n            raise ConstructorError(...)\n        value = self.construct_object(value_node, deep=deep)\n        mapping[key] = value\n    return mapping\n```\n\nSo yes, keys and values are constructed objects \u2014 with our custom constructors, they're Tokens. So `token.value` = {ScalarToken: ScalarToken}.\n\nAnd `DictToken._get_value()` (if it exists) converts to plain dict. Let me check via get_info.\n\nAlso `DictToken.get_value_for_key(key)` \u2014 I recall:\n\n```python\nclass DictToken(Token):\n    def get_value_for_key(self, key):\n        return self.value[key]\n```\n\nHmm, not sure it exists. Let me check.\n\nActually, from the typesystem source (v0.4.1), typesystem/tokenize/tokens.py:\n\n```python\nimport typing\nfrom typesystem.base import Position\n\n\nclass Token:\n    def __init__(self, value, start_index, end_index, content=None):\n        self.value = value\n        self.start_index = start_index\n        self.end_index = end_index\n        self.content = content\n\n    def _get_value(self):\n        if isinstance(self.value, dict):\n            return {\n                key if isinstance(key, str) else key.to_string(): value\n                for key, value in self.value.items()\n            }\n        elif isinstance(self.value, list):\n            return [\n                value._get_value() if isinstance(value, Token) else value\n                for value in self.value\n            ]\n        return self.value\n\n    def get_value(self):\n        return self._get_value()\n\n    @property\n    def start(self) -> Position:\n        line_no = self.content.count(\"\\n\", 0, self.start_index) + 1\n        column_no = self.start_index\n        if \"\\n\" in self.content[: self.start_index]:\n            column_no -= self.content.rfind(\"\\n\", 0, self.start_index) + 1\n        char_index = self.start_index\n        return Position(line_no, column_no, char_index)\n\n    @property\n    def end(self) -> Position:\n        line_no = self.content.count(\"\\n\", 0, self.end_index) + 1\n        column_no = self.end_index\n        if \"\\n\" in self.content[: self.end_index]:\n            column_no -= self.content.rfind(\"\\n\", 0, self.end_index) + 1\n        char_index = self.end_index\n        return Position(line_no, column_no, char_index)\n\n\nclass ScalarToken(Token):\n    def to_string(self) -> str:\n        return self.content[self.start_index : self.end_index + 1]\n\n\nclass DictToken(Token):\n    def _get_value(self):\n        return {\n            key if isinstance(key, str) else key.to_string(): value._get_value()\n            for key, value in self.value.items()\n        }\n\n    def get_value_for_key(self, key: Token) -> typing.Any:\n        return self.value[key]\n\n    def get_key_for_value(self, value: Token) -> typing.Any:\n        for key, item in self.value.items():\n            if item == value:\n                return key\n\n\nclass ListToken(Token):\n    def _get_value(self):\n        return [\n            value._get_value() if isinstance(value, Token) else value\n            for value in self.value\n        ]\n\n    def get_value_for_index(self, index: Token) -> typing.Any:\n        return self.value[index.value]\n```\n\nSomething like that. I'll verify with get_info.\n\nNow, Position equality: `Position.__eq__` compares line_no, column_no, char_index. So `error.position == Position(1, 1, 0)` works if attribute order matches: `Position(line_no, column_no, char_index)`. In the code under test: `Position(column_no=1, line_no=1, char_index=0)`. So `Position(line_no=1, column_no=1, char_index=0)`.\n\nLet me now write the get_info calls. I'll batch:\n\n1. get_info(\"tokenize_yaml\")\n2. get_info(\"_get_position\")\n3. get_info(\"Token\")\n4. get_info(\"ScalarToken\")\n5. get_info(\"DictToken\")\n6. get_info(\"ListToken\")\n7. get_info(\"ParseError\")\n8. get_info(\"Position\")\n\nThen finalize the test code.\n\nLet me also think about whether there might be an issue with `tokenize_yaml(\"key: value\")` and the `end` property vs `end_index`. I'll assert on `start_index`/`end_index` if those are the attribute names, or `start`/`end` if they're properties returning Position. Need get_info to confirm.\n\nAlso, one more test consideration: the `bytes` branch with non-UTF8 bytes: `tokenize_yaml(b\"\\xff\\xfe\")` \u2192 decode with \"ignore\" \u2192 \"\" \u2192 empty \u2192 ParseError \"No content.\" That exercises the decode-ignore path. Nice addition: `tokenize_yaml(b\"key: \\xffvalue\")` \u2192 decodes to \"key: value\". Let me include that in the bytes test or a separate one. Actually `b\"key: \\xffvalue\".decode(\"utf-8\", \"ignore\")` = \"key: value\". That's a nice test of the \"ignore\" error handler. I'll add it.\n\nHmm wait, careful: `b\"key: \\xffvalue\"` \u2014 the \\xff byte is ignored, giving \"key: value\". Then tokenize \u2192 DictToken. Good.\n\nNow, about the empty-content test with bytes: `tokenize_yaml(b\"\")` \u2192 decode \u2192 \"\" \u2192 ParseError. Good.\n\nLet me also think about whether `str_content.strip()` check: `\"  \\n  \"` \u2192 strip \u2192 \"\" \u2192 ParseError. Good.\n\nNow the ParseError assertions. Let me check ParseError's attributes via get_info. ParseError likely has `.text`, `.code`, `.position`, and maybe `.message` property. In typesystem/base.py:\n\n```python\nclass Message:\n    def __init__(self, text, code=None, index=0, position=None):\n        if position is None:\n            class Info:\n                line_no = index\n                column_no = index\n                char_index = index\n            position = Info()\n        self.text = text\n        self.code = code\n        self.position = position\n        self.start = position.column_no\n        self.end = position.column_no\n\n    def __eq__(self, other):\n        if self.__class__ != other.__class__:\n            return False\n        return (\n            self.text == other.text\n            and self.code == other.code\n            and self.index == other.index\n            and self.position == other.position\n        )\n\n    def __repr__(self):\n        return \"%s(text=%r, code=%r, index=%r, position=%r)\" % (\n            self.__class__.__name__,\n            self.text,\n            self.code,\n            self.index,\n            self.position,\n        )\n\n    def __str__(self):\n        if self.position.line_no == 1:\n            suffix = \"(line 1 column %d, char %d)\" % (self.position.column_no, self.position.char_index)\n        else:\n            suffix = \"(line %d column %d, char %d)\" % (...)\n        return f\"{self.text} {suffix}\"\n\n\nclass ParseError(Message, BaseException):\n    def __str__(self):\n        ...\n```\n\nHmm, I recall `Message` has `index` property? Let me check via get_info. Actually in newer typesystem:\n\n```python\nclass Message:\n    def __init__(self, text, code=None, index=0, position=None):\n        if position is None:\n            class Info:\n               