ef __eq__(self, other): ...\n    def __repr__(self): ...\n\nclass Message:\n    def __init__(self, text, code, index=0, position=None):\n        ...\n    def __eq__(self, other): ...\n    def __hash__(self): ...\n    def __repr__(self): ...\n\nclass ParseError(Message, BaseException):\n    def __init__(self, text, code, position):\n        super().__init__(text, code, position=position)  # hmm\n```\n\nActually:\n\n```python\nclass Message:\n    def __init__(self, text: str, code: str = None, index: int = 0, position: Position = None):\n        if position is None:\n            class Info:  # pragma: no cover\n                line_no = index\n                column_no = index\n                char_index = index\n            position = Info()\n        self.text = text\n        self.code = code\n        self.position = position\n        self.start = position.column_no\n        self.end = position.column_no\n\n    def __eq__(self, other):\n        return self.__hash__() == other.__hash__()\n\n    def __hash__(self):\n        ident = (self.code, self.index, self.text, self.start, self.end)\n        return hash(ident)\n\n    def __str__(self):\n        return f'{self.code} at char {self.index}: {self.text}'\n\n    def __repr__(self):\n        return f'{self.__class__.__name__}(text={self.text!r}, code={self.code!r}, index={self.index!r}, position={self.position!r})'\n\n\nclass ParseError(Message, BaseException):\n    ...\n```\n\nHmm, I don't remember exactly. Let me check with get_info.\n\nActually, in typesystem/base.py:\n\n```python\nclass Message:\n    def __init__(self, text, code=None, index=0, position=None):\n        if position is None:\n            class Info:\n                line_no = index\n                column_no = index\n                char_index = index\n            position = Info()\n        self.text = text\n        self.code = code\n        self.position = position\n        self.start = position.column_no\n        self.end = position.column_no\n```\n\nHmm, I'm not sure about `start`/`end`. Let me just use get_info.\n\nOK let me plan the test file:\n\n```python\nimport pytest\n\nfrom typesystem.base import ParseError, Position\nfrom typesystem.tokenize.tokens import DictToken, ListToken, ScalarToken\nfrom typesystem.tokenize import tokenize_yaml  # or from typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_yaml import tokenize_yaml\n```\n\nWait, is `tokenize_yaml` exported from `typesystem.tokenize`? In typesystem's repo, `typesystem/tokenize/__init__.py`:\n\n```python\nfrom .tokenize_json import tokenize_json\nfrom .tokenize_yaml import tokenize_yaml\n```\n\nHmm, actually I think it's:\n\n```python\nfrom typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_json import tokenize_json\nfrom typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_yaml import tokenize_yaml\n```\n\nEither way, `from typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_yaml import tokenize_yaml` works directly. I'll import from the specific module to also allow monkeypatching `yaml` on it.\n\nTests:\n\n1. `test_tokenize_yaml_bytes_content`: `tokenize_yaml(b\"key: value\")` returns DictToken; check `.value` dict, `.content`, start/end indices. Also verify key/value are ScalarTokens.\n\nWait \u2014 for `b\"key: value\"`, the DictToken: start=0, end = end_mark.index - 1. The end_mark for the mapping node... For a block mapping `key: value`, the end mark index is 10 (after \"value\"), so end-1 = 9. The content is \"key: value\" (10 chars). So DictToken(mapping, 0, 9, content=\"key: value\").\n\nThe mapping dict: keys are ScalarToken(\"key\", 0, 2), values ScalarToken(\"value\", 5, 9).\n\nHmm wait, for the key scalar `key`: start_mark.index=0, end_mark.index=3, so ScalarToken(\"key\", 0, 2). For value `value`: start=5, end=10 \u2192 ScalarToken(\"value\", 5, 9).\n\nTo verify: `token.get_value()` returns the dict. Then `list(token.value.keys())[0].to_string() == \"key\"`? Hmm, ScalarToken.to_string() returns `self.content[self.start : self.end + 1]`. Let me check ScalarToken:\n\n```python\nclass ScalarToken(Token):\n    def to_string(self):\n        return self.content[self.start : self.end + 1]\n```\n\nYes, I believe that's right.\n\nSo for the key token: content=\"key: value\", start=0, end=2 \u2192 to_string() = \"key\". Good.\n\nSimpler assertions: \n```python\ntoken = tokenize_yaml(b\"key: value\")\nassert isinstance(token, DictToken)\nassert token.start == 0\nassert token.end == 9\nassert token.content == \"key: value\"\nkey_token = list(token.value.keys())[0]\nvalue_token = token.value[key_token]\nassert key_token.to_string() == \"key\"\nassert value_token.to_string() == \"value\"\n```\n\nHmm, but `token.value` \u2014 Token has `value` attribute set in `__init__`? Let me check Token:\n\n```python\nclass Token:\n    def __init__(self, value, start_index, end_index, content=None):\n        self.value = value\n        self.start = start_index\n        self.end = end_index\n        self.content = content if content is not None else \"\"\n```\n\nHmm, I think Token stores `self.value = value`. And there's `get_value()`? Let me check with get_info.\n\nActually from typesystem/tokenize/tokens.py:\n\n```python\nclass Token:\n    def __init__(self, value, start_index, end_index, content=None):\n        self.value = value\n        self.start_index = start_index\n        self.end_index = end_index\n        self.content = content\n\n    def _get_value(self):\n        ...\n```\n\nHmm. I really need to check. Let me use get_info on Token, ScalarToken, DictToken, ListToken.\n\nFrom memory of the typesystem source (version 0.2.x / 3.x):\n\n```python\n# typesystem/tokenize/tokens.py\nfrom typesystem.base import Position\n\n\nclass Token:\n    def __init__(self, value, start_index, end_index, content=None):\n        self.value = value\n        self.start_index = start_index\n        self.end_index = end_index\n        self.content = content\n\n    def _get_value(self):\n        if isinstance(self.value, dict):\n            return {\n                key if isinstance(key, str) else key.to_string(): value\n                for key, value in self.value.items()\n            }\n        elif isinstance(self.value, list):\n            return [value._get_value() if isinstance(value, Token) else value for value in self.value]\n        return self.value\n\n    def get_value(self):\n        return self._get_value()\n\n    @property\n    def start(self) -> Position:\n        line_no = self.content.count(\"\\n\", 0, self.start_index) + 1\n        ...\n```\n\nHmm, I recall `start` and `end` are properties returning Position. Let me check via get_info.\n\nActually the real source (typesystem 0.4.x):\n\n```python\nclass Token:\n    def __init__(self, value, start_index, end_index, content=None):\n        self.value = value\n        self.start_index = start_index\n        self.end_index = end_index\n        self.content = content\n\n    def _get_value(self):\n        if isinstance(self.value, dict):\n            return {\n                key if isinstance(key, str) else key.to_string(): value\n                for key, value in self.value.items()\n            }\n        elif isinstance(self.value, list):\n            return [\n                value._get_value() if isinstance(value, Token) else value\n                for value in self.value\n            ]\n        return self.value\n\n    def get_value(self):\n        return self._get_value()\n\n    @property\n    def start(self) -> Position:\n        line_no = self.content.count(\"\\n\", 0, self.start_index) + 1\n        column_no = self.start_index\n        if \"\\n\" in self.content[: self.start_index]:\n            column_no -= self.content.rfind(\"\\n\", 0, self.start_index) + 1\n        char_index = self.start_index\n        return Position(line_no, column_no, char_index)\n\n    @property\n    def end(self) -> Position:\n        ...\n```\n\nAnd ScalarToken:\n\n```python\nclass ScalarToken(Token):\n    def to_string(self):\n        return self.content[self.start_index : self.end_index + 1]\n```\n\nAnd DictToken:\n\n```python\nclass DictToken(Token):\n    def _get_value(self):\n        return {\n            key if isinstance(key, str) else key.to_string(): value._get_value()\n            for key, value in self.value.items()\n        }\n\n    def get_value_for_key(self, key):\n        return self.value.get(key)  # hmm\n```\n\nHmm, actually:\n\n```python\nclass DictToken(Token):\n    def get_value_for_key(self, key: Token):\n        return self.value[key]\n```\n\nI'm not sure. Let me just use get_info to check all these.\n\nOK, plan: call get_info for:\n- `tokenize_yaml`\n- `_get_position`\n- `Token`\n- `ScalarToken`\n- `DictToken`\n- `ListToken`\n- `ParseError`\n- `Position`\n\nThese are all independent, so I can batch them.\n\nThen write the tests.\n\nLet me draft the tests now (to be refined after get_info):\n\n```python\nimport pytest\n\nimport typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_yaml as tokenize_yaml_module\nfrom typesystem.base import ParseError, Position\nfrom typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_yaml import tokenize_yaml\nfrom typesystem.tokenize.tokens import DictToken, ListToken, ScalarToken\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_requires_pyyaml(monkeypatch):\n    monkeypatch.setattr(tokenize_yaml_module, \"yaml\", None)\n    with pytest.raises(AssertionError) as exc_info:\n        tokenize_yaml(\"{}\")\n    assert \"'pyyaml' must be installed.\" in str(exc_info.value)\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_empty_string():\n    for content in (\"\", \"   \", b\"\", b\"  \\n \"):\n        with pytest.raises(ParseError) as exc_info:\n            tokenize_yaml(content)\n        error = exc_info.value\n        assert error.text == \"No content.\"\n        assert error.code == \"no_content\"\n        assert error.position == Position(column_no=1, line_no=1, char_index=0)\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_mapping():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(\"key: value\")\n    assert isinstance(token, DictToken)\n    assert token.content == \"key: value\"\n    assert token.start_index == 0\n    assert token.end_index == 9\n    assert token.get_value() == {\"key\": \"value\"}\n    (key_token,) = list(token.value.keys())\n    assert isinstance(key_token, ScalarToken)\n    assert key_token.to_string() == \"key\"\n    value_token = token.value[key_token]\n    assert isinstance(value_token, ScalarToken)\n    assert value_token.to_string() == \"value\"\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_bytes_content():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(b\"key: value\")\n    assert isinstance(token, DictToken)\n    assert token.content == \"key: value\"\n    assert token.get_value() == {\"key\": \"value\"}\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_sequence():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(\"- a\\n- b\")\n    assert isinstance(token, ListToken)\n    assert token.content == \"- a\\n- b\"\n    assert token.start_index == 0\n    assert token.end_index == 6\n    assert token.get_value() == [\"a\", \"b\"]\n    assert all(isinstance(item, ScalarToken) for item in token.value)\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_scalar_string():\n    token = tokenize_yaml('\"hello world\"')\n    assert isinstance(token, ScalarToken)\n    assert token.value == \"hello world\"\n    assert token.to_string() == '\"hello world\"'\n    assert token.start_index == 0\n    assert token.end_index == 12\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_int():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(\"123\")\n    assert isinstance(token, ScalarToken)\n    assert token.value == 123\n    assert token.to_string() == \"123\"\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_float():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(\"1.5\")\n    assert isinstance(token, ScalarToken)\n    assert token.value == 1.5\n    assert token.to_string() == \"1.5\"\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_bool():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(\"true\")\n    assert isinstance(token, ScalarToken)\n    assert token.value is True\n    assert token.to_string() == \"true\"\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_null():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(\"null\")\n    assert isinstance(token, ScalarToken)\n    assert token.value is None\n    assert token.to_string() == \"null\"\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_parse_error():\n    with pytest.raise