s DictToken(Token):\n    def _get_value(self):\n        return {\n            key if isinstance(key, str) else key.to_string(): value._get_value()\n            for key, value in self.value.items()\n        }\n\n    def get_value_for_key(self, key):\n        return self.value.get(key)  # hmm\n```\n\nHmm, actually:\n\n```python\nclass DictToken(Token):\n    def get_value_for_key(self, key: Token):\n        return self.value[key]\n```\n\nI'm not sure. Let me just use get_info to check all these.\n\nOK, plan: call get_info for:\n- `tokenize_yaml`\n- `_get_position`\n- `Token`\n- `ScalarToken`\n- `DictToken`\n- `ListToken`\n- `ParseError`\n- `Position`\n\nThese are all independent, so I can batch them.\n\nThen write the tests.\n\nLet me draft the tests now (to be refined after get_info):\n\n```python\nimport pytest\n\nimport typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_yaml as tokenize_yaml_module\nfrom typesystem.base import ParseError, Position\nfrom typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_yaml import tokenize_yaml\nfrom typesystem.tokenize.tokens import DictToken, ListToken, ScalarToken\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_requires_pyyaml(monkeypatch):\n    monkeypatch.setattr(tokenize_yaml_module, \"yaml\", None)\n    with pytest.raises(AssertionError) as exc_info:\n        tokenize_yaml(\"{}\")\n    assert \"'pyyaml' must be installed.\" in str(exc_info.value)\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_empty_string():\n    for content in (\"\", \"   \", b\"\", b\"  \\n \"):\n        with pytest.raises(ParseError) as exc_info:\n            tokenize_yaml(content)\n        error = exc_info.value\n        assert error.text == \"No content.\"\n        assert error.code == \"no_content\"\n        assert error.position == Position(column_no=1, line_no=1, char_index=0)\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_mapping():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(\"key: value\")\n    assert isinstance(token, DictToken)\n    assert token.content == \"key: value\"\n    assert token.start_index == 0\n    assert token.end_index == 9\n    assert token.get_value() == {\"key\": \"value\"}\n    (key_token,) = list(token.value.keys())\n    assert isinstance(key_token, ScalarToken)\n    assert key_token.to_string() == \"key\"\n    value_token = token.value[key_token]\n    assert isinstance(value_token, ScalarToken)\n    assert value_token.to_string() == \"value\"\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_bytes_content():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(b\"key: value\")\n    assert isinstance(token, DictToken)\n    assert token.content == \"key: value\"\n    assert token.get_value() == {\"key\": \"value\"}\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_sequence():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(\"- a\\n- b\")\n    assert isinstance(token, ListToken)\n    assert token.content == \"- a\\n- b\"\n    assert token.start_index == 0\n    assert token.end_index == 6\n    assert token.get_value() == [\"a\", \"b\"]\n    assert all(isinstance(item, ScalarToken) for item in token.value)\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_scalar_string():\n    token = tokenize_yaml('\"hello world\"')\n    assert isinstance(token, ScalarToken)\n    assert token.value == \"hello world\"\n    assert token.to_string() == '\"hello world\"'\n    assert token.start_index == 0\n    assert token.end_index == 12\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_int():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(\"123\")\n    assert isinstance(token, ScalarToken)\n    assert token.value == 123\n    assert token.to_string() == \"123\"\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_float():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(\"1.5\")\n    assert isinstance(token, ScalarToken)\n    assert token.value == 1.5\n    assert token.to_string() == \"1.5\"\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_bool():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(\"true\")\n    assert isinstance(token, ScalarToken)\n    assert token.value is True\n    assert token.to_string() == \"true\"\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_null():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(\"null\")\n    assert isinstance(token, ScalarToken)\n    assert token.value is None\n    assert token.to_string() == \"null\"\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_parse_error():\n    with pytest.raises(ParseError) as exc_info:\n        tokenize_yaml(\"a: b\\nc: 'd\")\n    error = exc_info.value\n    assert error.code == \"parse_error\"\n    assert error.text == \"found unexpected end of stream.\"\n    assert error.position.line_no == 2\n    assert error.position.char_index == 10\n    assert error.position.column_no == 6\n```\n\nWait, for the parse error test, let me double-check the problem message. For `a: 'd` unclosed single quote:\n\n```\nyaml.scanner.ScannerError: while scanning a quoted scalar\n  in \"<unicode string>\", line 2, column 5\nfound unexpected end of stream\n  in \"<unicode string>\", line 2, column 11\n```\n\nThe `problem` is \"found unexpected end of stream\" and `problem_mark` is at line 2, column 11 \u2192 index 10. So:\n- text = \"found unexpected end of stream.\"\n- position = _get_position(\"a: b\\nc: 'd\", index=10)\n  - line_no = content.count(\"\\n\", 0, 10) + 1 = 1 + 1 = 2\n  - column_no = 10 - content.rfind(\"\\n\", 0, 10) = 10 - 4 = 6\n  - char_index = 10\n\nGood.\n\nHmm, but wait \u2014 is `exc.problem` guaranteed to be \"found unexpected end of stream\"? In PyYAML's `scan_flow_scalar`:\n\n```python\ndef scan_flow_scalar(self, token_class):\n    ...\n    while True:\n        ...\n        if self.index == 5 ... \n```\n\nActually:\n\n```python\n            # Check if we are at the end of the stream.\n            if self.peek() == '':\n                raise ScannerError(\"while scanning a quoted scalar\", start_mark,\n                        \"found unexpected end of stream\", self.get_mark())\n```\n\nYes, that's the message. Stable across PyYAML versions (3.x through 6.x). Good.\n\nBut hold on \u2014 is the problem_mark the end of stream or the start? In `scan_flow_scalar`, the error is raised with `self.get_mark()` as the problem mark \u2014 the current position, which is the end of stream (index 10 for our content). Yes.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me re-check: `a: b\\nc: 'd` \u2014 length: \"a: b\" = 4 chars (indices 0-3), \"\\n\" at index 4, \"c: 'd\" = 5 chars (indices 5-9). Total length 10. End of stream index = 10. Yes.\n\nActually hmm, one more consideration: does the scanner reach the end of stream while scanning the quoted scalar? The quoted scalar starts at index 8 (`'`). It scans `d` then hits end of stream at index 10. Yes, problem_mark.index = 10.\n\nOK. But to be extra safe against version differences, maybe I should relax the position assertions slightly? The task says assertions should verify postconditions. I think asserting exact values is fine given stable PyYAML behavior. But let me hedge a little: assert `error.position.line_no == 2` and `error.position.char_index == 10`. These should be stable.\n\nAlternatively, I could use a simpler error input where I'm more certain. Let me think about `\"{\"`:\n\n```\n>>> yaml.load(\"{\", Loader=SafeLoader)\n```\nScanner: `{` \u2192 FlowMappingStartToken. Then end of stream. Parser: parse_flow_mapping_key: token is not `}` and not key... it calls parse_flow_node... which sees no token \u2192 ParserError \"while parsing a flow mapping ... expected '<document start>'...\"? Hmm, I don't remember. \n\nLet me stick with the unclosed quote.\n\nActually, you know what \u2014 let me also consider using `yaml.load` failure via a different route. The except clause catches `yaml.scanner.ScannerError` and `yaml.parser.ParserError`. The unclosed quote gives ScannerError. Good enough for the branch.\n\nNow, about `token.get_value()` for DictToken \u2014 I need to check what it returns. If DictToken._get_value converts keys via to_string and values via _get_value, then `token.get_value() == {\"key\": \"value\"}`. Let me verify with get_info.\n\nAlso for ListToken: `token.get_value() == [\"a\", \"b\"]`.\n\nFor the sequence `- a\\n- b`: content length 6. The ListToken: start=0, end = end_mark.index - 1. The block sequence end mark: after \"b\" at index 6? The end mark of the sequence node is at index 6 (end of stream). So end-1 = 5. Hmm wait: `- a\\n- b` \u2