`2021-01-01` resolves to timestamp tag, which has no constructor in CustomSafeLoader \u2192 would raise ConstructorError (not caught). But we don't need to test that.\n\nAlso note: for implicit resolution, `123` resolves to int tag \u2192 construct_int. Good.\n\nNow, one important consideration: does `construct_scalar` get called for the str tag? Yes: `add_constructor(DEFAULT_SCALAR_TAG, construct_scalar)` where DEFAULT_SCALAR_TAG = \"tag:yaml.org,2002:str\".\n\nNow, about the `assert yaml is not None` test with monkeypatch: the module does `try: import yaml ... except ImportError: yaml = None`. Actually wait, looking at the given code snippet, it starts with `import yaml` directly (the snippet shows `import yaml` at the top). But the real typesystem source has the try/except. Either way, monkeypatching `module.yaml = None` tests line 26's assert branch.\n\nHmm, but actually \u2014 is that a branch worth testing? Line 26 is just the assert; it's covered by any call. The \"branch\" of the assert failing is an exception path. Coverage-wise, line 26 is covered by any successful call. But adding the monkeypatch test makes the suite more thorough and covers the assert failure message. I'll include it \u2014 it's cheap and safe (monkeypatch reverts after test).\n\nWait, one concern: `monkeypatch.setattr(tokenize_yaml_module, \"yaml\", None)` \u2014 then `tokenize_yaml(\"{}\")` hits the assert. But note the function references the global `yaml` in its module namespace, so this works. Good.\n\nNow, about cleanup/state pollution: The CustomSafeLoader is defined inside the function, so no global state pollution. The monkeypatch is auto-reverted. Good.\n\nOne more consideration: importing `tokenize_yaml` \u2014 should I import from `typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_yaml` or `typesystem.tokenize`? The former is safer. Also for monkeypatching I need the module object: `import typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_yaml as tokenize_yaml_module`.\n\nHmm, wait. There's a potential issue: `typesystem/tokenize/__init__.py` might import tokenize_json which requires... no, tokenize_json doesn't require anything extra. Fine.\n\nAlso, pyyaml must be installed for the tests to pass (the module asserts it). The test environment presumably has pyyaml since the code under test requires it. I could add a pytest.importorskip(\"yaml\") guard, but that might skip tests unnecessarily. Actually, adding `pytest.importorskip` at module level would be defensive but if pyyaml is installed it's a no-op. Hmm, but if pyyaml is NOT installed, the import of the module itself succeeds (yaml=None fallback) and all tests would fail with AssertionError. Adding importorskip(\"yaml\") would skip gracefully. But the task says tests must be correct and improve coverage \u2014 presumably pyyaml is installed. I'll not add importorskip; actually, let me add it as a safety? Hmm. If pyyaml is missing, `tokenize_yaml` raises AssertionError for every call, and tests fail. With importorskip, they'd skip. I think it's cleaner without; the environment for coverage testing must have pyyaml. Actually, let me include `pytest.importorskip(\"yaml\")` \u2014 no wait, that could mask a real problem... The task is about covering lines 26-109, which requires pyyaml. I'll skip the importorskip and just import yaml normally in the test? No \u2014 I don't even need to import yaml in the test file. Let me keep it simple.\n\nHmm, actually, one thing: for the monkeypatch test, if pyyaml is not installed, `module.yaml` is already None and the assert fires anyway \u2014 the test would still pass! Interesting. But other tests would fail. Whatever \u2014 assume pyyaml installed.\n\nNow let me also think about whether to test `tokenize_yaml` with a nested structure to exercise more paths, e.g. `tokenize_yaml(\"key:\\n  - a\\n  - 1\")`. That would exercise construct_mapping, construct_sequence, construct_scalar, construct_int in one go. But separate tests are clearer.\n\nLet me also double check the DictToken value structure: `construct_mapping` returns `DictToken(mapping, start, end - 1, content=str_content)` where `mapping = loader.construct_mapping(node)`. Since the custom constructors are registered, the nested values are Tokens (ScalarToken etc.), not plain Python values. So `token.value` is `{ScalarToken(\"key\",0,2): ScalarToken(\"value\",5,9)}`.\n\nWait, is that right? `loader.construct_mapping(node)` \u2192 for each key/value node, calls `self.construct_object(key_node, ...)` which uses the registered constructor \u2192 returns ScalarToken. So yes, the dict keys/values are Tokens.\n\nHmm, but wait: `construct_mapping` in SafeLoader \u2014 does it deep-construct? `construct_mapping(self, node, deep=False)`: \n\n```python\ndef construct_mapping(self, node, deep=False):\n    if not isinstance(node, MappingNode):\n        raise ConstructorError(...)\n    mapping = {}\n    for key_node, value_node in node.value:\n        key = self.construct_object(key_node, deep=deep)\n        if not isinstance(key, collections.abc.Hashable):\n            raise ConstructorError(...)\n        value = self.construct_object(value_node, deep=deep)\n        mapping[key] = value\n    return mapping\n```\n\nSo yes, keys and values are constructed objects \u2014 with our custom constructors, they're Tokens. So `token.value` = {ScalarToken: ScalarToken}.\n\nAnd `DictToken._get_value()` (if it exists) converts to plain dict. Let me check via get_info.\n\nAlso `DictToken.get_value_for_key(key)` \u2014 I recall:\n\n```python\nclass DictToken(Token):\n    def get_value_for_key(self, key):\n        return self.value[key]\n```\n\nHmm, not sure it exists. Let me check.\n\nActually, from the typesystem source (v0.4.1), typesystem/tokenize/tokens.py:\n\n```python\nimport typing\nfrom typesystem.base import Position\n\n\nclass Token:\n    def __init__(self, value, start_index, end_index, content=None):\n        self.value = value\n        self.start_index = start_index\n        self.end_index = end_index\n        self.content = content\n\n    def _get_value(self):\n        if isinstance(self.value, dict):\n            return {\n                key if isinstance(key, str) else key.to_string(): value\n                for key, value in self.value.items()\n            }\n        elif isinstance(self.value, list):\n            return [\n                value._get_value() if isinstance(value, Token) else value\n                for value in self.value\n            ]\n        return self.value\n\n    def get_value(self):\n        return self._get_value()\n\n    @property\n    def start(self) -> Position:\n        line_no = self.content.count(\"\\n\", 0, self.start_index) + 1\n        column_no = self.start_index\n        if \"\\n\" in self.content[: self.start_index]:\n            column_no -= self.content.rfind(\"\\n\", 0, self.start_index) + 1\n        char_index = self.start_index\n        return Position(line_no, column_no, char_index)\n\n    @property\n    def end(self) -> Position:\n        line_no = self.content.count(\"\\n\", 0, self.end_index) + 1\n        column_no = self.end_index\n        if \"\\n\" in self.content[: self.end_index]:\n            column_no -= self.content.rfind(\"\\n\", 0, self.end_index) + 1\n        char_index = self.end_index\n        return Position(line_no, column_no, char_index)\n\n\nclass ScalarToken(Token):\n    def to_string(self) -> str:\n        return self.content[self.start_index : self.end_index + 1]\n\n\nclass DictToken(Token):\n    def _get_value(self):\n        return {\n            key if isinstance(key, str) else key.to_string(): value._get_value()\n            for key, value in self.value.items()\n        }\n\n    def get_value_for_key(self, key: Token) -> typing.Any:\n        return self.value[key]\n\n    def get_key_for_value(self, value: Token) -> typing.Any:\n        for key, item in self.value.items():\n            if item == value:\n                return key\n\n\nclass ListToken(Token):\n    def _get_value(self):\n        return [\n            value._get_value() if isinstance(value, Token) else value\n            for value in self.value\n        ]\n\n    def get_value_for_index(self, index: Token) -> typing.Any:\n        return self.value[index.value]\n```\n\nSomething like that. I'll verify with get_info.\n\nNow, Position equality: `Position.__eq__` compares line_no, column_no, char_index. So `error.position == Position(1, 1, 0)` works if attribute order matches: `Position(line_no, column_no, char_index)`. In the code under test: `Position(column_no=1, line_no=1, char_index=0)`. So `Position(line_no=1, column_no=1, char_index=0)`.\n\nLet me now write the get_info calls. I'll batch:\n\n1. get_info(\"tokenize_yaml\")\n2. get_info(\"_get_position\")\n3. get_info(\"Token\")\n4. get_info(\"ScalarToken\")\n5. get_info(\"DictToken\")\n6. get_info(\"ListToken\")\n7. get_info(\"ParseError\")\n8. get_info(\"Position\")\n\nThen finalize the test code.\n\nLet me also think about whether there might be an issue with `tokenize_yaml(\"key: value\")` and the `end` property vs `end_index`. I'll assert on `start_index`/`end_index` if those are the attribute names, or `start`/`end` if they're properties returning Position. Need get_info to confirm.\n\nAlso, one more test consideration: the `bytes` branch with non-UTF8 bytes: `tokenize_yaml(b\"\\xff\\xfe\")` \u2192 decode with \"ignore\" \u2192 \"\" \u2192 empty \u2192 ParseError \"No content.\" That exercises the decode-ignore path. Nice addition: `tokenize_yaml(b\"key: \\xffvalue\")` \u2192 decodes to \"key: value\". Let me include that in the bytes test or a separate one. Actually `b\"key: \\xffvalue\".decode(\"utf-8\", \"ignore\")` = \"key: value\". That's a nice test of the \"ignore\" error handler. I'll add it.\n\nHmm wait, careful: `b\"key: \\xffvalue\"` \u2014 the \\xff byte is ignored, giving \"key: value\". Then tokenize \u2192 DictToken. Good.\n\nNow, about the empty-content test with bytes: `tokenize_yaml(b\"\")` \u2192 decode \u2192 \"\" \u