.modules['a.b.c']`? Or `X = a.b.c` (attribute lookup)? \n\nPer PEP 328 / actual behavior: `import a.b.c as X` does `X = getattr(a.b, 'c')` after importing a.b.c. Hmm, actually since Python 3.7, there was a change (bpo-30024): `import a.b.c as X` now binds X to `sys.modules['a.b.c']`... no wait.\n\nLet me think carefully. Before Python 3.7: `import a.b.c as X` was equivalent to `import a.b.c; X = a.b.c` (attribute access on the parent package). If the parent package's `__init__` shadows the submodule name with a function, then `X` would be the function!\n\nIn Python 3.7+, bpo-30024 fixed `import a.b as X` to use `sys.modules` lookup... Actually the fix in 3.7: \"import a.b as X\" now first checks sys.modules['a.b'] before falling back to getattr(a, 'b'). Hmm, I believe the change was: `import a.b as X` binds X to sys.modules['a.b'] if present, else getattr. Let me recall bpo-30024: \"import module.submodule as X\" \u2014 the fix made it equivalent to `X = importlib.import_module('a.b.c')`? \n\nFrom CPython changelog: \"bpo-30024: Circular imports involving absolute imports with binding a submodule to a name are now supported.\" \u2014 the change: `import a.b as X` now looks up `sys.modules['a.b']` first. Yes, in Python 3.7+, `import a.b.c as X` binds X to the module from sys.modules, not the (possibly shadowed) attribute.\n\nHmm, but actually I recall the fix applies to the `as` form: \"When a submodule is imported with `import a.b as b`, the name b is now bound to sys.modules['a.b']\". Let me double check: bpo-30024 fixed in 3.7: \"import a.b as c\" now first checks if 'a.b' is in sys.modules. Yes.\n\nSo `import typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_yaml as module` binds the module in Python 3.7+. But to be safe across versions, I could use `import importlib; module = importlib.import_module(\"typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_yaml\")` or `sys.modules`. Or simpler: use `pytest.MonkeyPatch` via fixture and `importlib.import_module`. \n\nActually, simplest robust approach:\n\n```python\nimport sys\nimport typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_yaml\n\ndef test_...(monkeypatch):\n    monkeypatch.setattr(sys.modules[\"typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_yaml\"], \"yaml\", None)\n```\n\nOr even simpler: `from typesystem.tokenize import tokenize_yaml as tokenize_yaml_func` and monkeypatch on `tokenize_yaml_func.__globals__`:\n\n```python\nmonkeypatch.setitem(tokenize_yaml.__globals__, \"yaml\", None)\n```\n\nHmm, `monkeypatch.setattr(tokenize_yaml, \"__globals__\"...)` no. `monkeypatch.setitem(tokenize_yaml.__globals__, \"yaml\", None)` works \u2014 setitem on the globals dict, and monkeypatch restores it after. That's clean and robust. But mutating a module's globals via setitem \u2014 monkeypatch.setitem saves the old value and restores it. \n\nActually, cleanest: \n\n```python\nimport typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_yaml as tokenize_yaml_module\n```\n\nIn Python 3.7+ this binds the module correctly. The typesystem library supports Python 3.7+ (typesystem 0.4 requires Python 3.7? Actually typesystem supports 3.6/3.7+). Hmm. To be maximally safe, I'll use `sys.modules`:\n\n```python\nimport sys\nimport typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_yaml  # noqa: F401  (ensures module is imported)\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_requires_pyyaml(monkeypatch):\n    module = sys.modules[\"typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_yaml\"]\n    monkeypatch.setattr(module, \"yaml\", None)\n    ...\n```\n\nThat's robust. Or use importlib. I'll go with sys.modules approach. Actually, `import typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_yaml` alone ensures it's in sys.modules. Then `sys.modules[...]` gets the module. \n\nAlternatively, since the function object's `__globals__` IS the module dict:\n\n```python\nfrom typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_yaml import tokenize_yaml\n\ndef test_...(monkeypatch):\n    monkeypatch.setitem(tokenize_yaml.__globals__, \"yaml\", None)\n```\n\nThis is the most direct and robust. monkeypatch.setitem on the globals dict \u2014 restores after test. I like this. But hmm, is `__globals__` of the function the module dict? Yes, functions defined at module level have `__globals__` == module.__dict__. \n\nI'll use that approach. Actually, let me use both belt-and-suspenders? No, one is fine. I'll use `monkeypatch.setitem(tokenize_yaml.__globals__, \"yaml\", None)`.\n\nHmm, wait \u2014 but there's a subtlety: `monkeypatch.setitem` requires the key to exist? No, setitem works even if key doesn't exist (it records that it wasn't there and removes it on undo). The key \"yaml\" exists in the module globals. Fine.\n\nNow, let me also think about whether the tests could interfere with each other via the CustomSafeLoader \u2014 no, it's local to each call.\n\nAnother consideration: `yaml.load(str_content, CustomSafeLoader)` \u2014 in PyYAML 6, `yaml.load` requires Loader as positional or keyword; here it's positional, fine.\n\nNow, let me also double check the float test: \"1.5\" \u2192 construct_yaml_float \u2192 1.5. And to_string() = \"1.5\". Good.\n\nEdge: \"1.5\" resolver float regex matches. Good.\n\nNow, about asserting `token.value == 123` for int \u2014 construct_yaml_int returns int 123. Good.\n\nLet me now also consider adding a test for a document with mixed content to exercise construct_mapping with nested sequence, ensuring DictToken/ListToken interplay:\n\n```python\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_nested():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(\"items:\\n  - 1\\n  - 2\")\n    assert isinstance(token, DictToken)\n    assert token.get_value() == {\"items\": [1, 2]}\n```\n\nThis exercises construct_mapping \u2192 construct_sequence \u2192 construct_int. Nice for robustness. The get_value: DictToken._get_value converts keys via to_string and values via _get_value (ListToken._get_value \u2192 [ScalarToken.value...]). So {\"items\": [1, 2]}. \n\nLet me compute indices for assertions: content \"items:\\n  - 1\\n  - 2\" \u2014 length: \"items:\" = 6 (0-5), \"\\n\" = 6, \"  - 1\" = indices 7-11, \"\\n\" = 12, \"  - 2\" = indices 13-17. Total length 18. DictToken end = 17. Hmm, I could a