e 26: `assert yaml is not None, \"'pyyaml' must be installed.\"` \u2014 this executes whenever the function is called, so any test covers it. But to be thorough, could also test that when yaml is None, the assert fails. That would require monkeypatching the module's `yaml` attribute to None. That's a nice branch coverage test:\n\n```python\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_requires_pyyaml(monkeypatch):\n    import typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_yaml as module\n    monkeypatch.setattr(module, \"yaml\", None)\n    with pytest.raises(AssertionError) as exc_info:\n        module.tokenize_yaml(\"{}\")\n    assert \"'pyyaml' must be installed.\" in str(exc_info.value)\n```\n\nWait, but the assert message: `assert yaml is not None, \"'pyyaml' must be installed.\"` \u2014 when it fails, the AssertionError message is \"'pyyaml' must be installed.\" Yes.\n\nHmm, but careful: the module might do `try: import yaml except ImportError: yaml = None`. The module-level `yaml` name is what the function references. Monkeypatching `module.yaml = None` works.\n\nBut wait \u2014 there's a subtlety: if the module was imported with `from typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_yaml import tokenize_yaml`, the function's globals are the module's dict, so monkeypatching the module attribute works.\n\nNow, bytes handling: `tokenize_yaml(b\"key: value\")` \u2192 decodes to str. Test that it returns a DictToken.\n\nEmpty string: `tokenize_yaml(\"\")` \u2192 ParseError with text=\"No content.\", code=\"no_content\", position=Position(column_no=1, line_no=1, char_index=0). Also `tokenize_yaml(\"   \")` (whitespace only) and `tokenize_yaml(b\"\")`.\n\nNow let me also think about whether the ParseError's string representation or attributes matter. ParseError has `message` property, `code`, `text`... Let me check ParseError:\n\n```python\nclass Position:\n    def __init__(self, line_no, column_no, char_index):\n        self.line_no = line_no\n        self.column_no = column_no\n        self.char_index = char_index\n    def