s in (self.yaml_implicit_resolvers, ...):\n            for tag in ...:\n                if tag is None: continue\n                if regexp match: return tag\n    if kind == ScalarNode:\n        implicit_tag = self.DEFAULT_SCALAR_TAG  # actually returns self.DEFAULT_SCALAR_TAG\n```\n\nYes, returns DEFAULT_SCALAR_TAG = \"tag:yaml.org,2002:str\". Good.\n\nNow \u2014 one subtle thing about `construct_scalar` for the str tag: `loader.construct_scalar(node)` returns the scalar value as str. Good.\n\nNow let me also double check `tokenize_yaml(\"123\")`: resolver matches int regex \u2192 tag \"tag:yaml.org,2002:int\" \u2192 construct_int \u2192 `loader.construct_yaml_int(node)` \u2192 123. ScalarToken(123, 0, 2, content=\"123\"). to_string() = \"123\". Good.\n\n`tokenize_yaml(\"1.5\")` \u2192 float tag \u2192 construct_yaml_float \u2192 1.5. Good.\n\n`tokenize_yaml(\"true\")` \u2192 bool tag \u2192 construct_yaml_bool \u2192 True. Good.\n\n`tokenize_yaml(\"null\")` \u2192 null tag \u2192 construct_yaml_null \u2192 None. Good.\n\nHmm, wait: is it \"null\" or should I use \"~\"? Both resolve to null tag. \"null\" is fine.\n\nNow, one potential gotcha: in PyYAML, `construct_yaml_float` for \"1.5\" returns 1.5. Good.\n\nAnother gotcha: for \"123\", the resolver's int regex in PyYAML 6 handles \"123\" fine.\n\nNow, the ListToken test: `tokenize_yaml(\"- a\\n- b\")`. Actually, hmm \u2014 wait. Let me reconsider: does the block sequence node's end_mark.index equal 7 (end of stream)? The parser creates the SequenceNode with start_mark at the first `-` (index 0) and end_mark after the last item. The end mark for the block sequence... In `parse_block_sequence_entry`, when BlockEndToken is found, `node.end_mark = token.end_mark`. The BlockEnd token's end_mark is at the end of stream (index 7). So end = 7 - 1 = 6. Good.\n\nSimilarly for the mapping \"key: value\": BlockEnd token at end of stream (index 10) \u2192 end = 9. Good.\n\nAnd for scalar \"hello\": the ScalarNode's end_mark is at index 5 (after \"hello\") \u2192 end = 4. Good.\n\nNow, `token.get_value()` for ListToken: `_get_value` converts nested tokens. For `- a\\n- b`, value = [ScalarToken(\"a\",2,2), ScalarToken(\"b\",6,6)] \u2192 get_value() = [\"a\", \"b\"]. Good.\n\nFor DictToken \"key: value\": get_value() = {\"key\": \"value\"}. Good.\n\nNow, let me also add a test that exercises `get_value_for_key` / `get_value_for_index`? Not necessary for coverage of tokenize_yaml.py. Keep tests focused but with meaningful assertions.\n\nLet me also think about whether to test `tokenize_yaml` returning the token for a nested structure to exercise multiple constructors in one call \u2014 e.g.:\n\n```python\ntoken = tokenize_yaml(\"key:\\n  - 1\\n  - true\")\n```\n\nThis exercises construct_mapping, construct_sequence, construct_int, construct_bool. But individual tests already cover each. I'll keep separate tests for clarity.\n\nNow, about the \"no top-level code calling pytest.main\" requirement \u2014 fine, just test functions.\n\nLet me also consider: the tests import `from typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_yaml import tokenize_yaml`. For the monkeypatch test, I need the module: `import typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_yaml as yaml_tokenize_module`. \n\nHmm, one concern: `typesystem.tokenize.__init__` might import `tokenize_json` which imports `json` \u2014 fine.\n\nWait, actually there's another consideration: does `typesystem/tokenize/__init__.py` exist and import things? In typesystem 0.4.x:\n\n```python\n# typesystem/tokenize/__init__.py\nfrom typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_json import tokenize_json\nfrom typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_yaml import tokenize_yaml\n\n__all__ = [\"tokenize_json\", \"tokenize_yaml\"]\n```\n\nImporting `typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_yaml` triggers `typesystem.tokenize.__init__` which imports tokenize_json \u2014 fine, no heavy deps.\n\nAlso `typesystem/__init__.py` imports various things \u2014 fine.\n\nNow, let me write the get_info calls.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the exact attribute names on Token. I'll call get_info for Token, ScalarToken, DictToken, ListToken, ParseError, Position, tokenize_yaml, _get_position. That's 8 calls in one block.\n\nAfter getting info, I'll write the final test script.\n\nLet me draft the final test script now (subject to adjustment):\n\n```python\nimport pytest\n\nimport typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_yaml as tokenize_yaml_module\nfrom typesystem.base import ParseError, Position\nfrom typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_yaml import tokenize_yaml\nfrom typesystem.tokenize.tokens import DictToken, ListToken, ScalarToken\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_asserts_pyyaml_installed(monkeypatch):\n    monkeypatch.setattr(tokenize_yaml_module, \"yaml\", None)\n    with pytest.raises(AssertionError) as exc_info:\n        tokenize_yaml(\"key: value\")\n    assert \"'pyyaml' must be installed.\" in str(exc_info.value)\n\n\n@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"content\", [\"\", \"   \", \" \\n\\t \", b\"\", b\" \\n \"])\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_no_content(content):\n    with pytest.raises(ParseError) as exc_info:\n        tokenize_yaml(content)\n    error = exc_info.value\n    assert error.text == \"No content.\"\n    assert error.code == \"no_content\"\n    assert error.position == Position(line_no=1, column_no=1, char_index=0)\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_bytes_are_decoded():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(b\"key: value\")\n    assert isinstance(token, DictToken)\n    assert token.content == \"key: value\"\n    assert token.get_value() == {\"key\": \"value\"}\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_bytes_invalid_utf8_ignored():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(b\"key: \\xffvalue\")\n    assert isinstance(token, DictToken)\n    assert token.content == \"key: value\"\n    assert token.get_value() == {\"key\": \"value\"}\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_mapping():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(\"key: value\")\n    assert isinstance(token, DictToken)\n    assert token.content == \"key: value\"\n    assert token.start_index == 0\n    assert token.end_index == 9\n    assert token.get_value() == {\"key\": \"value\"}\n    (key_token,) = token.value.keys()\n    assert isinstance(key_token, ScalarToken)\n    assert key_token.to_string() == \"key\"\n    value_token = token.value[key_token]\n    assert isinstance(value_token, ScalarToken)\n    assert value_token.to_string() == \"value\"\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_sequence():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(\"- a\\n- b\")\n    assert isinstance(token, ListToken)\n    assert token.content == \"- a\\n- b\"\n    assert token.start_index == 0\n    assert token.end_index == 6\n    assert token.get_value() == [\"a\", \"b\"]\n    assert all(isinstance(item, ScalarToken) for item in token.value)\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_string_scalar():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(\"hello\")\n    assert isinstance(token, ScalarToken)\n    assert token.value == \"hello\"\n    assert token.to_string() == \"hello\"\n    assert token.start_index == 0\n    assert token.end_index == 4\n    assert token.content == \"hello\"\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_int_scalar():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(\"123\")\n    assert isinstance(token, ScalarToken)\n    assert token.value == 123\n    assert token.to_string() == \"123\"\n    assert token.start_index == 0\n    assert token.end_index == 2\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_float_scalar():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(\"1.5\")\n    assert isinstance(token, ScalarToken)\n    assert token.value == 1.5\n    assert token.to_string() == \"1.5\"\n    assert token.start_index == 0\n    assert token.end_index == 2\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_bool_scalar():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(\"true\")\n    assert isinstance(token, ScalarToken)\n    assert token.value is True\n    assert token.to_string() == \"true\"\n    assert token.start_index == 0\n    assert token.end_index == 3\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_null_scalar():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(\"null\")\n    assert isinstance(token, ScalarToken)\n    assert token.value is None\n    assert token.to_string() == \"null\"\n    assert token.start_index == 0\n    assert token.end_index == 3\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_parse_error():\n    with pytest.raises(ParseError) as exc_info:\n        tokenize_yaml(\"a: b\\nc: 'd\")\n    error = exc_info.value\n    assert error.code == \"parse_error\"\n    assert error.text == \"found unexpected end of stream.\"\n    assert error.position.line_no == 2\n    assert error.position.column_no == 6\n    assert error.position.char_index == 10\n```\n\nWait \u2014 for the parse error test, I should double check `error.text`. `text = exc.problem + \".\"` \u2192 \"found unexpected end of stream\" + \".\" = \"found unexpected end of stream.\". Good.\n\nHmm, but is the problem message exactly \"found unexpected end of stream\"? In PyYAML's scan_flow_scalar:\n\n```python\n                if self.peek() == '':\n                    raise ScannerError(\"while scanning a quoted scalar\", start_mark,\n                            \"found unexpected end of stream\", self.get_mark())\n```\n\nYes. And this has been stable since PyYAML 3.x. Good.\n\nBut wait \u2014 actually, I want to double-check the flow for `a: b\\nc: 'd`:\n- Scanner: plain scalar `a` (simple key), `:` value token, plain scalar `b`, then line break, plain scalar `c` (simple key), `:` value token, then `'` starts single-quoted scalar at index 8. Scans `d` (index 9), then index 10 is end of stream \u2192 ScannerError(\"while scanning a quoted scalar\", start_mark=index 8, \"found unexpected end of stream\", mark=index 10).\n\nSo `exc.problem = \"found unexpected end of stream\"`, `exc.problem_mark.index = 10`. \n\n_get_position(\"a: b\\nc: 'd\", 10):\n- line_no = \"a: b\\nc: 'd\".count(\"\\n\", 0, 10) + 1 = 1 + 1 = 2\n- column_no = 10 - \"a: b\\nc: 'd\".rfind(\"\\n\", 0, 10) = 10 - 4 = 6\n- char_index = 10\n\nGood.\n\nHmm, wait \u2014 I need to double check `_get_position`'s implementation. Let me recall from typesystem/tokenize/tokenize_yaml.py... Actually, `_get_position` might be defined in tokenize_yaml.py itself:\n\n```python\ndef _get_position(content: str, index: int) -> Position:\n    return Position(\n        line_no=content.count(\"\\n\", 0, index) + 1,\n        column_no=index - content.rfind(\"\\n\", 0, index),\n        char_index=index,\n    )\n```\n\nYes, I believe that's it. I'll verify with get_info.\n\nNow, about `error.position == Position(line_no=1, column_no=1, char_index=0)` \u2014 Position defines `__eq__`? Let me check via get_info. If not, I'll compare attributes individually. To be safe, I could compare attributes individually:\n\n```python\nassert (error.position.line_no, error.position.column_no, error.position.char_index) == (1, 1, 0)\n```\n\nThat's safe regardless of __eq__. But if Position has __eq__, the direct comparison is nicer. I'll check get_info.\n\nSimilarly for Message/ParseError \u2014 does it have `__eq__`? Not needed.\n\nNow, about `token.start_index` / `token.end_index` vs `token.start` / `token.end`: need get_info to confirm. If `start`/`end` are properties returning Position, then `token.start_index` is the raw int. Let me check.\n\nAlso `token.value` \u2014 the raw value. And `token.get_value()` \u2014 the converted value. And `token.content`.\n\nLet me also double-check ScalarToken.to_string: `self.content[self.start_index : self.end_index + 1]`. For \"hello\" (0,4): content[0:5] = \"hello\". Good.\n\nOne more potential issue: for `tokenize_yaml(\"hello\")`, is the returned token's `value` \"hello\"? construct_scalar returns `loader.construct_scalar(node)` = \"hello\". Yes.\n\nNow \u2014 hmm, wait. There's a subtlety with ScalarToken for the str tag: the ScalarNode for a plain scalar \"hello\" has start_mark at index 0 and end_mark at index 5. So ScalarToken(\"hello\", 0, 4). Good.\n\nFor \"123\": ScalarNode start 0, end 3 \u2192 ScalarToken(123, 0, 2). Good.\n\nNow let me also think: are there any other branches in lines 26-109? \n\n- Line 28 `if isinstance(content, bytes)`: True (bytes test) and False (str tests). \u2713\n- Line 33 `if not str_content.strip()`: True (empty tests) and False (parse tests). \u2713\n- Lines 38-101: executed on any non-empty parse. \u2713\n- Line 103-104: try/yield. \u2713\n- Line 105-109: except path (parse error test). \u2713\n\nAll covered by my planned tests.\n\nOne more: the `assert yaml is not None` \u2014 line 26 covered by all tests; the failing branch covered by the monkeypatch test.\n\nNow, potential issue with the monkeypatch test: after `monkeypatch.setattr(tokenize_yaml_module, \"yaml\", None)`, calling `tokenize_yaml(\"key: value\")` raises AssertionError. But wait \u2014 could the module have been imported such that `tokenize_yaml`'s globals point to a different module object? No \u2014 `from typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_yaml import tokenize_yaml` gives the function whose `__globals__` is the module dict of `typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_yaml`. And `import typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_yaml as tokenize_yaml_module` gives the same module object. So setattr works.\n\nHmm, but one gotcha: if `typesystem.tokenize.__init__` does `from .tokenize_yaml import tokenize_yaml`, then `typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_yaml` might be the FUNCTION, not the module! Let me check: in `typesystem/tokenize/__init__.py`:\n\n```python\nfrom typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_json import tokenize_json\nfrom typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_yaml import tokenize_yaml\n```\n\nIf so, `typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_yaml` attribute is the function (shadowing the submodule). But `import typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_yaml as X` \u2014 how does Python resolve this? `import a.b.c as X` imports `a.b.c` module and binds X to the module... Actually, in Python, `import a.b.c as X` binds X to `a.b.c` module \u2014 but there's a subtlety: it uses getattr on the parent package after importing. In Python 3.7+, `import a.b.c as X` is equivalent to importing a.b.c and then `X = getattr(a.b, 'c')` \u2014 wait no.\n\nActually, the semantics: `import a.b.c as X` first imports `a.b.c` (the module), then binds `X = sys.modul